+     *
+     * However, real-world applications - notably the custom VMR9
+     * allocator/presenters used by Visual Novel engines such as KiriKiri -
+     * routinely pass VMR9AllocFlag_TextureSurface together with
+     * VMR9AllocFlag_OffscreenSurface when allocating YUV video surfaces
+     * (e.g. NV12, YV12). They rely on the allocator returning a texture
+     * surface so the surface can be sampled by a pixel shader for
+     * YUV-to-RGB colour conversion.
+     *
+     * Rejecting that combination forced such applications to fall back to
+     * a plain offscreen surface, which cannot be bound as a texture. As a
+     * result the downstream rendering pipeline raised an exception and the
+     * video never played.
+     *
+     * To fix this, the combination is now honoured by allocating a texture
+     * surface (its top-level surface can be retrieved via
+     * IDirect3DBaseTexture9::GetSurfaceLevel and used as either a texture
+     * or an offscreen plain surface, so it is a strict superset of either
+     * flag requested alone). The branches below use bitwise tests instead
+     * of equality so additional bits in dwFlags (e.g.
+     * VMR9AllocFlag_RGBDynamicSwitch, VMR9AllocFlag_DXVATarget) no longer
+     * cause the request to be silently dropped through to the
+     * "Unhandled flags" fallback. */
 
